Tasks Completed

This week I did research only. I did not write any code. I studied the interpolation methods I am responsible for so that I understand what my module must do in the group project:

1. Linear interpolation: estimating a value between two known points by drawing a straight line through them. This is the simplest case and the starting point for the other methods.
2. Lagrange interpolation: building the curve through all known points as a sum of each y-value multiplied by a weight that depends on the query point.
3. Newton interpolation: building the same curve using divided-difference coefficients, which can be calculated once and reused, and which make it easy to add a new point.
4. Polynomial interpolation:finding the single polynomial of degree n-1 that passes through n points. I learned that Lagrange and Newton are two ways of writing this same polynomial, so their answers must agree.
I also worked out what my role covers: the files `interpolation.hpp` and `interpolation.cpp`, the interpolation tests, and individual evidence for Lagrange, Newton and polynomial interpolation. I also noted how interpolation connects to the satellite project: estimating a satellite's position between the time steps computed by the solver.

